{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# J4 — Pin a `UserIdentityModel` contract (accountId for Cloud, name for Data Center)\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nUser identity is the second real Cloud/DC difference. Jira Cloud identifies users by opaque `accountId`\n(username and userkey were removed for GDPR); Data Center identifies them by `name`. This leaks into two places\nin the Cloud adapter: `OutboundMapper.resolve_assignee`, which returns\n`(value, authoritative, is_account_id)` and encodes the 3-state account-resolution fast-path the core diff\nconsults (ADR 0035 §(d) canonical-comparison corollary), and the transport's create/update assignee payload\nshape.\n\nPR #120 handled this by copying `resolve_assignee` and hardcoding `is_account_id=False`, and its copy consulted\n`getattr(self, \"_assignee_resolver\", None)` — an attribute nothing ever sets, so its entire authoritative\nbranch was unreachable and DC would have returned `authoritative=False` for every assignee. That is not a\ncosmetic bug: a permanently non-authoritative assignee resolution makes the outbound diff re-emit assignee\nchanges it can never converge, which is the churn class epic `ace2` was created to fix.\n\n## Approach\n\nDefine a `UserIdentityModel` Protocol in `adapters/jira_family/` [rebar:62d9-9f6d-ecf4-4743] with `resolve(local_value,\nremote_identity)` returning the existing `(value, authoritative, is_account_id)` tuple, and\n`to_payload(value)` returning the deployment's assignee field shape. Two implementations: `AccountIdIdentity`\n(Cloud — the current behavior verbatim, `{\"accountId\": …}`) and `NameIdentity` (DC — resolves through the DC\nuser-search endpoint, `{\"name\": …}`).\n\n`NameIdentity` performs a **real** lookup against DC's user-search endpoint rather than echoing the local\nvalue, so it can return `authoritative=True` and the diff converges. Its resolver is injected at construction\nfrom the DC transport [rebar:9fd4-a94c-156e-4a56], not discovered via `getattr`, so there is no unreachable branch.\n\n**Alternative rejected:** DC returns the local value with `authoritative=False` always (PR #120's effective\nbehavior). It compiles and passes shallow tests while guaranteeing assignee-diff churn on every pass.\n\n**Alternative rejected:** normalizing both to a single \"user reference\" string. The core diff's 3-state\nfast-path genuinely needs to know whether the value is an accountId, so flattening it would push the branch\nback into core.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_family/identity_model.py` (new).\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/backend.py` — Cloud constructed with `AccountIdIdentity`.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_user_identity_model.py` (new).\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit tier with a stateful fake resolver for the lookup (the irreducible external boundary), plus a shared\ncontract test over both implementations, since the 3-state contract must hold identically for each.\n\n- shared contract, parameterized over both models: an empty local value yields\n  `(\"\", False, <model default>)`; a value that resolves to the SAME identity the remote already has yields\n  `(None, True, …)` (converged, nothing to emit); a value resolving to a different identity yields that\n  identity with `authoritative=True`.\n- Cloud-specific: the resolved value is an accountId and `is_account_id is True`; payload is `{\"accountId\": …}`.\n- DC-specific: the resolved value is a username and `is_account_id is False`; payload is `{\"name\": …}`.\n- failure path: an unresolvable assignee raises `BackendAssigneeNotFoundError` (the neutral base at\n  `_backend.py:48`) rather than silently returning a non-authoritative value.\n- regression oracle for PR #120's defect: a test asserts that for a resolvable assignee, DC returns\n  `authoritative=True` — this test fails against the `getattr`-based implementation.\n- contrast case: an unassigned local value yields the documented desired-unassigned state, not a lookup.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] one parameterized contract test covers all three resolution states for both identity models\n- [ ] a test asserts DC returns `authoritative=True` for a resolvable assignee (the anti-churn oracle)\n- [ ] a test asserts an unresolvable assignee raises `BackendAssigneeNotFoundError` for both models\n- [ ] `grep -rn '_assignee_resolver' src/` returns nothing — no `getattr`-discovered collaborator anywhere\n- [ ] J1's pinned Cloud assignee behavior is unchanged (`make test` green with those assertions unmodified)"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785010372605879001,"uuid":"257ad801-2dbd-4c73-bb22-ddcd9496f916"}